window.docContents[129].resolve({"/Foundations/Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Tempered-Distributions--Distributional-support":{"contents":"A distribution-like functional vanishes on a set  when it annihilates\nevery test function whose topological support lies in . The definition\nallows arbitrary . The complement of its support is the union of all\nopen vanishing sets; equivalently, distributional support is the\nintersection of all closed sets whose complements are vanishing sets:Mathlib's definition is abstract enough to apply to both classical and\ntempered distributions. The probe space is the one present in the functional\nitself: ordinary distributions are tested by , while tempered\ndistributions are tested by .\n\nClosedness is the property that lets support arguments pass to complements and\nwork with open vanishing sets.\n\nThe distributional support of every distribution is closed:\n\nEvery set appearing in the defining intersection is closed, and an arbitrary\nintersection of closed sets is closed.\n\nThe derivative support law is the tempered counterpart of the statement for\nordinary distributions, and the one-term case of the constant-coefficient\nsupport theorem.\n\nFor  with  a complex normed space,\ndistributional differentiation cannot enlarge support:\n\nIf  vanishes on an open set, then it annihilates the derivative of every\ntest function supported there because differentiation preserves the support\ninclusion. The derivative distribution therefore vanishes on every open set\nwhere  vanishes, which gives the support inclusion.\n\nThe Dirac mass is the test case in which the support is computed exactly rather\nthan bounded. The inclusion in  uses only vanishing away from ; the\nreverse inclusion needs a Schwartz function not vanishing at  with support\ninside an arbitrary neighborhood, which is where finite-dimensionality of \nis used.\n\nIn finite dimensions the support of the tempered Dirac distribution at  is exactly the\nsingleton :\n\nThe Dirac mass vanishes on the complement of , so its support is\ncontained in that singleton. Every neighborhood of  contains a smooth\ncompactly supported function that is nonzero at ; evaluating the Dirac\nmass on this function proves the reverse inclusion.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Distributions and Fourier Analysis\u0009Tempered Distributions","header":"3.4.2.5. Distributional support","id":"/Foundations/Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Tempered-Distributions--Distributional-support"},"/Foundations/PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators--Cauchy-problems--The-formal-solution":{"contents":"The analytic theorem is proved for a first-order system in normal form. Time\nis the coordinate , the unknown is  of\n, and\n\n\n\nA general analytic Cauchy problem reduces to this one by subtracting the\ninitial datum and adjoining time as an extra unknown; the\nanalytic evolution theorem\nperforms that reduction. Here the coefficients  and  are\nformal power series in the  variables , and the unknown is a\ntuple of formal series in .\n\nOver any commutative ring  containing , and for any formal coefficient series\n and , there is exactly one tuple of formal series\n with zero coefficient of  andwhere  substitutes the series  for the state variables.\nThe underlying recursion is stated for any order  and any index set: if\nthe coefficients of  up to time degree  depend only on the\ncoefficients of  below time degree , then\n has a unique solution whose first  time\ncoefficients vanish.\n\nGroup the coefficients of a series by their power of . Since the unknowns\nvanish at , substitution into the coefficient series is admissible,\nand the coefficient of  on the right depends only on the coefficients\nof  of time degree at most , spatial derivatives included, because\n does not change the power of . The equation therefore\ndefines the coefficient of  in  from those already chosen.\nStrong induction on the time degree gives existence, and the same induction\nshows that any two solutions agree degree by degree.\n\nThe recursion is where the order of the equation in time matters. For the\nheat equation the right-hand side  takes two spatial derivatives,\nyet it uses only lower time degrees, so a unique formal solution exists\nthere too; it simply fails to converge. Existence of a formal solution is\ntherefore cheap, and everything depends on estimating its coefficients.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009PDE Problems and Differential Operators\u0009Cauchy problems","header":"3.2.4.2. The formal solution","id":"/Foundations/PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators--Cauchy-problems--The-formal-solution"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Difference-quotients":{"contents":"For every finite exponent, the global forward theory is complete:\n\n* the difference quotient is a bounded linear operator on the global\n model;* Sobolev membership gives the standard quotient bound by the corresponding\nweak derivative;* as the step tends to zero, difference quotients converge strongly to the\nweak derivative in ;* localization by a compactly supported cutoff transports the estimate to an\ninterior region of a domain.\n\nFor , the converse recovers a weak gradient from uniformly\nbounded local difference quotients, with the same bound. It holds on\narbitrary open domains. Reflexivity supplies a weakly convergent subsequence;\ntesting its limit against smooth compactly supported functions identifies\nthe derivative. The endpoint  cannot be added to this statement:\nbounded difference quotients can describe a function of bounded variation\nwith a jump.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Sobolev Coverage and Open Problems","header":"4.6.1.7. Difference quotients","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Difference-quotients"}});